{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And he disabled</b>. There is no difference between וַיַחֲלִישׁ and וַיֶחֱלָשׁ (Job 14:10). Just as יִטְרֹף (Psalms 7:3) is like יִטְרָף (Genesis 49:27); תִּדֹּר נֶדֶר (Numbers 30:4) and וַיִּדַּר יַעֲקֹב (Genesis 28:20) [are alike], with pataḥ only; this is the analogy of a word like שֶׁם, sometimes in construct and sometimes absolute. Or: \"Would a mighty man die and be disabled?\" — and he was already disabled in the usual way; \"and it bred worms and stank\" (above 16:20). Therefore he mentioned \"a mighty man, a warrior.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "Reading ויחלש in the lemma as the hifil of ח-ל-ש (to disable/weaken). The argument about שם sometimes being in construct (נסמך) and sometimes absolute (נפרד) is rendered literally; Ibn Ezra draws an analogy about words that alternate between forms."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:25.571967480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}